Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp\footnote{Under corpus micro-F1, LP $\Delta{=}+0.09{\pm}0.03$\,pp; the larger instance-averaged gains concentrate on few-entity instances.}, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, LP $\Delta$ collapses from $+1.39$ to $+0.09$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

Signal ranking is task-specific; per-type analysis shows MV degradation concentrates in low-F1 types ($\rho{=}0.90$, $p{=}0.002$; Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across 23 configurations ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; $n{=}23$, insufficient for reliable correlation---we treat this as supplementary evidence; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

MV gains range from $-2.71$\,pp (Few-NERD) to $+0.6$\,pp (CoNLL at $N{=}64$; Figure~\ref{fig:n_scaling}); the optimal strategy is dataset-dependent (LP-best $+1.4$\,pp on Few-NERD vs.\ MV $+0.61$\,pp on CoNLL).
The framework extends to RE, where $40$\% zero-F1 from upstream entity span errors creates a distinct failure mode---\textit{diversity among failures} rather than quality-differentiated outputs---despite comparable degeneracy ($10.1$\% vs.\ SciERC $13.4$\%; Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Signal rankings are robust across temperatures, seeds, and $N$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).
Qwen3-4B QE matches 8B ($|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$); LoRA rank${=}8$ lowers degeneracy but LP stays below greedy (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).
The gap persists from 4B to 72B zero-shot to GPT-5.5 (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

Given that sample-level selection fails uniformly (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), we ask whether entity-level aggregation can extract value from multi-sample generation under favorable diagnostic conditions.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction (exploratory).}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}).
The diagnostic framework predicts gains when degeneracy and LP alignment are both favorable.
LP-weighted construction yields $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp on Few-NERD ($p{<}0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}), consistent with this prediction ($D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp), a smaller non-significant $+0.32$\,pp on SciERC, and no gain on CoNLL ($-0.39$\,pp).\footnote{Degenerate instances contribute $\Delta{=}0$ by construction; gains concentrate in the non-degenerate subset ($+3.36$\,pp SciERC, $+5.18$\,pp CoNLL, $+7.08$\,pp Few-NERD).}
We group the 57 comparisons into methodological families---per-sample signals ($k{=}21$), cross-sample signals ($k{=}26$), and entity construction ($k{=}10$)---reflecting mechanistic distinctions defined at experiment design time (not selected post-hoc to optimize $p$-values).
Under global Holm--Bonferroni \cite{holm1979simple} correction across all $k{=}57$ comparisons, $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (suggestive); under per-family correction within the 10-comparison entity-construction family, $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$ (significant); BH FDR: $6/10$ construction comparisons at FDR${<}0.05$ (Appendix~\ref{app:holm}).
We report all three corrections for transparency; the family partition was not pre-registered.
Construction and LP selection are complementary ($\rho_{\text{adj}}$: Few-NERD $0.516$); QE signals support selective prediction ($+5$--$8$\,pp at 80\% coverage; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\paragraph{Training convergence modulates scaling returns.}
Training convergence state is itself a determinant of SC efficacy: 5-epoch training on Few-NERD eliminates LP gains entirely (LP selection: $+1.39{\to}+0.09{\pm}0.04$\,pp, 94\% reduction; entity construction: $+0.88{\to}+0.20$\,pp, 77\% reduction; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
The diagnostic framework is consistent with this observation: 5-epoch constant-F1 degeneracy rises to $31$\% (vs.\ $13$\% at 3 epochs), degrading conditions for both selection and construction.\footnote{Degeneracy rates vary by definition: constant-F1 degeneracy (all $N$ samples yield identical F1) gives $31$\%/$13$\%; entity-set exact-match degeneracy gives $26$\%/$9$\%. The directional pattern is consistent.}
This suggests SC may complement early stopping strategies---under-converged models preserve output diversity that enables aggregation gains, while fully converged models exhibit degeneracy that renders scaling ineffective.

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, degeneracy ($12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking.
The degeneracy decomposition (\S\ref{sec:theory}) identifies $\alpha$---not degeneracy---as the binding constraint ($8$--$33{\times}$ tighter), providing a diagnostic tool for localizing failure rather than a predictive bound.
This capability-diversity tradeoff \cite{yun2025price} persists across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}), scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}), and decoding modes---LP compression is model-intrinsic rather than an artifact of constrained decoding (LP std ratio $0.86$--$1.00{\times}$ across three datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}).
Compared to \citet{yao2026consistency}'s task-level plateau, we provide instance-level decomposition.

\paragraph{Scale and outlook.}
The gap persists from 4B fine-tuned to 72B zero-shot and GPT-5.5, with degeneracy varying by model family rather than scale (Qwen2.5: $32$--$46$\%, Qwen3: $13$\%, GPT-5.5: $50$\%; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
The MV advantage persists at $N{=}16$ for pretrained models ($+4.1$\,pp, capturing $29$\% of oracle headroom), though it plateaus rather than scales---oracle headroom expands ($11.2{\to}14.1$\,pp) while MV gains remain flat, suggesting a voting aggregation ceiling even when diversity is available (Table~\ref{tab:pretrained_summary}).
Self-verification via entailment prompting proves ineffective (AUROC $\approx$ 0.50; Appendix~\ref{app:esj}); whether improved calibration can overcome signal alignment collapse remains open.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
The diagnostic framework---localizing failure to degeneracy and signal alignment collapse---is our primary contribution; cross-task and cross-model extensions are exploratory.
Practitioners can use degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment at $N{=}8$ as a lightweight pre-deployment check: high degeneracy ($>50$\%) indicates scaling will not help; low degeneracy with strong alignment may justify entity construction; otherwise, greedy decoding remains optimal.

